Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, naturalistic faces and fabric; candlelight, deep shadows, 1840s interiors.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

A cramped 1840s bedchamber. Heavy shutters sealed tight. A narrow bed. Thick darkness broken only by a thin seam of light under the door.

(SOUND: distant floorboard creak, wind pressing the shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

A lean man in dark period coat and waistcoat—NARRATOR, thirties, pale, sharp-eyed, hair slicked back—stands unseen beyond the door, listening.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

(SOUND: a pocket watch ticking, very close)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad?

INT. HOUSE HALLWAY - NIGHT

NARRATOR holds a dark lantern closed tight against his chest. His hand trembles once, then steadies on the latch of the chamber door.

(SOUND: latch metal whispering under fingers)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning light seeps past the shutters. The OLD MAN sits up in bed—elderly, thin white hair, one pale blue eye filmed like a vulture's. NARRATOR enters boldly, smiling, a basin in his hands.

(SOUND: shutters knocked by wind, basin water sloshing)

NARRATOR
And how have you passed the night?

The Old Man answers with a weak nod. Narrator sets the basin down, kind, attentive. The filmed eye catches the light. Narrator's smile freezes a fraction.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

Narrator turns away, jaw tight, the kindness still fixed on his face.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

INT. HOUSE HALLWAY - NIGHT

Midnight. Narrator at the chamber door again, lantern shut. He turns the latch with exquisite care.

(SOUND: latch easing, house settling in the cold)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

He opens the door a hand's width and slides the closed lantern through, then his head—slow, slow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Narrator's head in the opening. He undoes the lantern a hair. A single thin ray crawls across the floor and finds the bed.

(SOUND: lantern hinge creaks once)

The Old Man sleeps. The vulture eye is closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him. And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

Narrator withdraws. The ray dies. Darkness returns.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Daybreak. Narrator strides in, hearty, calling out.

(SOUND: curtains yanked, birds faint beyond the shutters)

NARRATOR
Good morning! And how have you passed the night?

The Old Man blinks his filmed eye at him. Narrator clasps his shoulder, smiling wide.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. HOUSE HALLWAY - NIGHT

The eighth night. Narrator at the door, face bright with triumph. He eases the latch. The door moves by degrees.

(SOUND: a watch's tick; the door edge kissing the frame)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

He chuckles under his breath—barely a breath.

(SOUND: a soft involuntary chuckle)

Inside, bedclothes rustle hard.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Pitch black. Shutters fastened. The Old Man sits bolt upright in bed, staring into nothing.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

Narrator's head is already in the room. He freezes. The lantern stays shut.

(SOUND: silence so hard it rings; a single bed-rope creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

The Old Man remains sitting up, listening. Narrator does not breathe.

(SOUND: death-watch beetles ticking faintly in the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was still sitting up in the bed listening;—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A low stifled groan escapes the Old Man—terror, not pain.

(SOUND: a stifled groan from the bed)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. I knew the sound well.

Narrator's mouth twitches—pity and a private chuckle at once.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Still later. The Old Man has not lain down. Narrator's fingers find the tin fastening of the lantern.

(SOUND: thumb slipping on tin)

A single dim ray, thin as spider-thread, shoots out and falls full upon the open vulture eye—wide, dull blue, veiled, hideous.

(MUSIC: low tense drone under a rising pulse)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it.

Nothing else of the face is lit—only the eye.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick sound—as a watch muffled in cotton)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense?—now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound.

Narrator holds the ray dead still on the eye. He scarcely breathes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man's heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

(SOUND: the muffled heartbeat growing quicker, louder)

The sound swells. Narrator's eyes widen. Sweat stands on his brow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder still; a neighbour's distant dog barks once)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell Narrator throws the lantern wide open and leaps into the room. Light blasts the bed.

(SOUND: yell; lantern clatter; bed ropes scream)

The Old Man shrieks once—once only. Narrator drags him to the floor and hauls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: body striking boards; bed frame slamming down; one cut-off shriek)

Narrator stands over the wreckage, smiling gaily.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

(SOUND: muffled heartbeat under the bed—slowing)

Narrator waits, head cocked. The sound thins.

(SOUND: the muffled beat falters... then ceases)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

Narrator drags the bed aside. He kneels, places a hand on the still chest, and holds it there.

(SOUND: nothing from the body; Narrator's own breath harsh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Later. A tin tub on the boards. Narrator works in grim silence by lantern light—dismembering, wrapping. No blood reaches the floor.

(SOUND: saw and cloth; liquid caught in the tub)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

He pries up three planks. Deposits the bundles between the scantlings. Replaces the boards flush, cunning, seamless.

(SOUND: planks levered up; boards tapped home)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong.

He wipes his hands. Surveys the clean floor. A short, sharp laugh.

NARRATOR
Ha! ha!

(SOUND: a church bell far off striking four)

INT. STREET DOOR - NIGHT

Still dark. A firm knocking at the street door. Narrator descends with a light heart and opens.

(SOUND: iron knocker; door hinges)

Three men in period coats and badges—OFFICER REYNOLDS, solid and calm; OFFICER HALE, younger, watchful; OFFICER BRIGGS, broad-shouldered—stand on the step with perfect suavity.

(SOUND: night street hush behind them)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour. We are to search the premises.

Narrator smiles and bows them in.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen—welcome. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

INT. HOUSE HALLWAY - NIGHT

Narrator leads the three officers through with open hands, urging them on.

(SOUND: boots on boards; doors opened wide)

NARRATOR
Search—search well. I bid you.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Lanterns and candlelight. Narrator shows the undisturbed room—the old man's treasures neat on a chest. He carries in chairs himself.

(SOUND: chairs set down; floorboards dull and solid underfoot)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I brought chairs into the room, and desired them here to rest from their fatigues.

He places his own chair dead center—upon the very spot above the hidden corpse—and sits.

NARRATOR
Rest from your fatigues. You see? All is well.

Officer Reynolds, Officer Hale, and Officer Briggs sit. They glance about, satisfied. Narrator answers them cheerily.

(SOUND: pleasant low conversation; a chair leg scrapes)

The officers chat of familiar things. Narrator's smile holds. Then a faint change—he pales. His fingers find his temple.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

(SOUND: a thin ringing, then a low dull quick pulse under it)

Narrator talks more freely, voice bright. The officers nod, still at ease.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct:—it continued and became more distinct... until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

(SOUND: the muffled-watch heartbeat—steady, growing)

Narrator goes very pale. He speaks faster, higher.

NARRATOR
More tea?—the weather—the street is quiet tonight, is it not?

(SOUND: heartbeat louder)

He gasps once for breath. The officers hear nothing; they smile on.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not. I talked more quickly—more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased.

Narrator surges to his feet. He argues about trifles, gesturing hard.

(SOUND: heartbeat still rising; his boots pacing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations; but the noise steadily increased.

He paces heavy strides. Foams. Raves. Grabs his chair and grates it on the boards over the hidden place.

(SOUND: chair legs screeching on planks; heartbeat drowning all)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards, but the noise arose over all and continually increased.

The three officers chat pleasantly and smile. Narrator's face breaks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!

(SOUND: heartbeat pounding—louder—louder—louder)

Narrator cannot bear their calm faces another second. He screams and points at the floor beneath his chair.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

(SOUND: the heartbeat thundering; chair crashing aside)

The officers spring up. Narrator falls to his knees on the guilty boards, still pointing, eyes wild.

(MUSIC: a final dissonant swell cutting to silence)

FADE OUT.

THE END
